1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Who is waiting on whom? Blocking, diagnosis, and why a deadlock is different

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:13,700
Two separate connections, each served by its own backend process (PID).

3
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:18,400
A changes the priority and stops, without COMMIT or ROLLBACK.

4
00:00:18,900 --> 00:00:21,400
B wants the same row. Different column, same row: B waits for A.

5
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:25,800
A plain SELECT would not wait; it reads the committed version (low).

6
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,300
A stuck connection can't inspect itself. A third session observes.

7
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:34,600
Read the waiter's row: 7332's blocking list is {7310}. B waits for A.

8
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:38,100
A is 'idle in transaction': idle, yet holding the row. Its empty list ≠ holds no locks.

9
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:42,800
A PID identifies a server process, not a user or a ticket. These numbers are illustrative.

10
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:47,600
Killing B, the waiter, fixes nothing: A would still hold the row.

11
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:51,600
Decided case: A rolls back. B finishes. Final row: low, in_progress.

12
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:55,300
Both endings let B finish, but they keep different data. Report the final row, not the spinner.

13
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:02,400
A has updated 1001. B has updated 1002.

14
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:05,400
Now each wants the other's row. A waits for B, and B waits for A.

15
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:09,900
Neither can finish by waiting. PostgreSQL detects the cycle.

16
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:13,400
It aborts one transaction (here B) so the other can continue. Which one is not up to you.

17
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:16,900
Retry the WHOLE aborted transaction. Taking rows in one consistent order prevents this cycle.

18
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:28,500
Remember: Two writers on the same row: the second one waits. pg_blocking_pids(waiter) names the blocker. Direction matters. 'idle in transaction' can be the one holding everyone up. Ending the blocker decides the data: check the final row. A deadlock is a cycle: PostgreSQL aborts one; retry the whole transaction.

